<%# The pictures the agent sent during one turn, drawn as a strip rather than a
    column: a few screenshots are often wider than the column, and growing the
    answer downwards pushes the conversation out of view. The strip scrolls
    sideways, which keeps every picture the same height and the thread the same
    width.

    Each picture is a button, not a link: opening it is a moment in the page,
    not a navigation. The download anchor beside it is a link, because that is
    what a download is — the same URL the lightbox offers, so both paths hand
    back the file the reader is looking at.

    The whole timeline is redrawn on every event of a running turn, and a
    picture that is rebuilt with it is fetched and decoded again — five of them
    blink in step with the answer. So each one carries its stored id and asks
    htmx to keep the node it already has: the picture stays exactly as drawn
    while the text under it grows. -%>
<div class="attachments" role="group" aria-label="Gambar dari Rubot">
  <% images.each do |image| -%>
  <figure class="attachment"
          <% if image[:id] %>id="picture-<%= h(image[:id]) %>" hx-preserve="true"<% end %>>
    <button type="button"
            class="attachment-open"
            data-lightbox="<%= h(image[:url]) %>"
            data-name="<%= h(image[:name]) %>"
            aria-label="Buka <%= h(image[:name]) %>">
      <img src="<%= h(image[:url]) %>" alt="<%= h(image[:name]) %>" loading="lazy">
    </button>
    <a class="attachment-download"
       href="<%= h(image[:url]) %>"
       download="<%= h(image[:name]) %>"
       title="Unduh <%= h(image[:name]) %>"
       aria-label="Unduh <%= h(image[:name]) %>">↓</a>
  </figure>
  <% end -%>
</div>
